Reach the fallback for DA1 and XTVERSION

Both of these answer a question about WHICH TERMINAL this is, and
libvterm answers them for itself -- reasonably, since it has no way to
know what is built on top of it. The result is that an application
embedding libvterm cannot correct either one, and both are wrong in the
same way for every such application:

  - Primary DA answers "?1;2c", a bare VT100 with the advanced video
    option. That is a claim about capabilities, and it is the
    conventional place to advertise sixel (parameter 4). An application
    that decodes sixel has no way to say so, so every client that probes
    for it the standard way is told there is none.

  - XTVERSION answers ">|libvterm(0.3)". Anything keying off it believes
    it is talking to libvterm rather than to the program the user
    actually ran, which is precisely what XTVERSION exists to prevent.

This is the same shape as 0002 and 0004: libvterm answers a question the
layer above it knows better, and answers it with the one reply that
stops a client asking again. The fix is the same too -- offer the
sequence to vterm_screen_set_unrecognised_fallbacks first, in its own
shape, and answer as before only when the fallback declines.

DA1 is handed to the fallback with no leader and command 'c'; XTVERSION
with leader ">" and command 'q'. An application therefore matches on
exactly what it would have matched on had the sequence arrived
unrecognised, which is 0004's rule and the reason these read the same
way in the consuming code.

Only the val == 0 form of DA1 is offered. "CSI >0c" and the parameterised
forms are different questions, and libvterm already declines them.

Applied on top of 0001-0005 at build time (see the top-level Makefile);
upstream is otherwise untouched. Worth sending upstream alongside 0002
and 0004.

--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -1302,8 +1302,20 @@ static int on_csi(const char *leader, const long args[], int argcount, const cha
   case 0x63: // DA - ECMA-48 8.3.24
     val = CSI_ARG_OR(args[0], 0);
     if(val == 0)
+    {
+      /* The layer above knows what this terminal can actually do -- most
+       * usefully whether it decodes sixel, which is advertised here as
+       * parameter 4 and cannot be added from outside. Offer the query
+       * before answering for it. */
+      if(state->fallbacks && state->fallbacks->csi) {
+        long fallback_args[1] = { 0 };
+        if((*state->fallbacks->csi)("", fallback_args, 1, "", 'c', state->fbdata))
+          break;
+      }
+
       // DEC VT100 response
       vterm_push_output_sprintf_ctrl(state->vt, C1_CSI, "?1;2c");
+    }
     break;
 
   case LEADER('>', 0x63): // DEC secondary Device Attributes
@@ -1463,6 +1475,15 @@ static int on_csi(const char *leader, const long args[], int argcount, const cha
     break;
 
   case LEADER('>', 0x71): // XTVERSION - xterm query version string
+    /* The name of the program the user ran, not the name of this
+     * library. An embedder that answers for itself is what XTVERSION is
+     * for; one that cannot is worse than one that says nothing, because
+     * the answer it gives is confidently wrong. */
+    if(state->fallbacks && state->fallbacks->csi) {
+      if((*state->fallbacks->csi)(">", NULL, 0, "", 'q', state->fbdata))
+        break;
+    }
+
     request_version_string(state);
     break;
 
